  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The variant cleanly separates transport from interpretation, so UDP-feed v0 could plausibly carry it without leaking binding details. But the specimen stops at envelope mechanics; it does not define the session/group state or other behavior needed to show more than a UDP round trip, so scenario fit is only moderate.",
    "strengths": [
      "Opaque payload bytes plus pCID dispatch preserve a clear boundary between UDP-feed transport and higher-layer meaning.",
      "The draft explicitly avoids a central pCID registry and supports sparse-knowledge operation through visible unsupported handling.",
      "Canonical DAG-CBOR bytes and mandatory signature protocol slots are reasonably auditable and implementable."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No concrete session or group composition contract with UDP-feed v0 is specified.",
      "Signature coverage stops at [pcid, payload], leaving replay or session binding to another layer.",
      "The positional draft format is less self-describing and is not yet frozen."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Best-effort inspection of unknown payloads could drift into accidental trust of unverified data.",
      "Relay or hop-local evidence placement across wrapper, payload, and signature conventions may diverge between implementations."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What exact session-layer metadata above UDP-feed v0 must be carried here versus inside the payload protocol?",
      "How should invalid or unsupported sig_pcid cases be recorded locally for audit and recovery?",
      "What pCID discovery and migration path avoids de facto registry centralization over long horizons?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
